                <h2>Split the Market Before Launching</h2>
<p>Do not treat Southeast Asia or Latin America as a single advertising region. Separate markets by language, payment behavior, device usage, service availability, and customer intent. Prioritize countries according to creative requirements, purchase value, conversion path, and account risk. Start with controlled budget rather than covering every country at once. Exclude locations where the product cannot be sold, delivered, or supported.</p>
<h2>Creative Review: Translation Alone Is Not Localization</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep ad copy, landing pages, and support messages aligned with the same product and offer details.</li>
<li>Review imagery, gestures, colors, social roles, and everyday consumption contexts rather than merely translating the text.</li>
<li>Have native speakers check slang, tone, abbreviations, dates, units, prices, and calls to action.</li>
<li>Recreate vertical videos for mobile viewing and keep subtitles and buttons inside safe areas.</li>
<li>Use separate copy, covers, and landing-page variants for different language groups. Keep a small controlled test for a shared concept, but do not scale unadapted creative.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, a utility app used one English video across several markets. Views were stable, yet the landing-page exit rate remained high because the demo did not match local expectations. The team revised the instructions and tested new creative before scaling.</p>
<h2>Accounts and Payments: Validate the Funding Path First</h2>
<ul>
<li>Ensure the ad account, payment card, settlement currency, invoice entity, and contracting entity match the approved company information.</li>
<li>Complete a small test recharge before increasing limits. Verify balance visibility, charges, failed-payment refunds, and billing records.</li>
<li>Set tiered balance alerts based on average daily spend. Keep a buffer for payment failures and time-zone differences.</li>
<li>Remember that campaign budget limits do not replace account-level balance management.</li>
<li>Record every recharge order, payment time, currency, fee, exchange rate, and linked ad account. These details simplify reconciliation and exception tracking.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Attribution and Finance: Establish One Reporting Standard</h2>
<ul>
<li>Define each conversion event, including its name, trigger condition, and deduplication rule.</li>
<li>Record the original transaction currency, settlement currency, and platform-converted value. Do not compare figures in different currencies without conversion.</li>
<li>State the time zone used by the ad platform, analytics tool, finance system, and daily reports.</li>
<li>Investigate duplicate conversions, delayed event delivery, incorrect landing-page domains, missing UTM parameters, and active test events.</li>
<li>Combine platform data with local business metrics such as valid registrations, purchases, refunds, and customer support issues. Do not judge performance using installs alone.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Final Pre-Launch Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li>Are the target country, language, and landing page aligned?</li>
<li>Does the creative fit local context and mobile formats?</li>
<li>Are prices, promotions, and product availability accurate?</li>
<li>Do privacy disclosures, consent flows, and data handling meet local requirements?</li>
<li>Have the account balance, payment method, and recharge records been verified?</li>
<li>Are conversion events, currencies, time zones, and attribution rules consistent?</li>
<li>Are small-scale testing, balance alerts, and abnormal-spend controls in place?</li>
</ol>
<h2>Common Mistakes and Corrections</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Common Practice</th>
<th>Risk</th>
<th>Recommended Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>One generic creative for a country</td>
<td>Weak relevance and conversion</td>
<td>Build separate creative groups by language and audience</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Copying a global ad account</td>
<td>Payment and entity confusion</td>
<td>Verify the account, currency, and documentation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Reporting only platform installs</td>
<td>Overstated conversion quality</td>
<td>Review purchases and refunds as well</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Treating budget limits as account balance</td>
<td>Unexpected ad stoppage</td>
<td>Use daily-spend and balance alerts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Comparing expenses in different currencies</td>
<td>Reconciliation gaps</td>
<td>Store original values, exchange rates, and converted amounts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Reusing seasonal creative indefinitely</td>
<td>Missed opportunities or poor relevance</td>
<td>Maintain a local seasonal creative calendar</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Localization should be part of the launch workflow, not a post-publication fix. Enter these checks into a shared launch sheet and require review from media, creative, customer support, finance, and compliance teams. Test large recharges, sensitive categories, and new data-processing arrangements on a limited scale before full rollout.</p>
